WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.060
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about AWS Batch.</v>

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.550
So you can run your Batch jobs as Docker images,

3
00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.410
and you have two options to run them.

4
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.140
Either you choose a managed compute environment

5
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.690
in which you're going to either dynamically provision

6
00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.680
your instances using EC2 and Spot Instances in the VPC,

7
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.020
or you choose to rely on AWS Fargate,

8
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.450
which is a full serverless offering,

9
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.640
or you choose an unmanaged compute environment

10
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.940
in which you provision yourself the ECS cluster

11
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.830
or the EKS cluster.

12
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.020
Then you're going to define the optimal quantity

13
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.460
and type based on your volume

14
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.200
and requirements for your Batch jobs,

15
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.400
and you're going to only pay

16
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.410
for the underlying resources used.

17
00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.320
Batch itself is a service that you don't pay for.

18
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.960
For example, using Batch, you can do Batch processing

19
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.410
of images, or you can run thousands of concurrent jobs.

20
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.030
You can schedule these Batch jobs

21
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.070
by using Amazon EventBridge,

22
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.510
or you can orchestrate them using AWS Step Functions.

23
00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.670
So let's look at a possible solution architecture

24
00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.700
for creating, for example, some thumbnails

25
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.710
out of images from Amazon S3.

26
00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.690
So in Batch, we're going to create a Batch job

27
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.850
and it's going to be managed by ECS,

28
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.950
and it could be either instance EC2 instances,

29
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.930
Spot Instances, so as a Spot Fleet,

30
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.410
or using Fargate in the serverless fashion.

31
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.900
Now, as a user, we want to upload files into Amazon S3,

32
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.340
they're going to be images.

33
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.270
Now, how do we go from Amazon S3 to triggering a Batch job?

34
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.103
Well, two options.

35
00:01:22.103 --> 00:01:25.800
Number one, we can use the Amazon S3 event notifications

36
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.540
and trigger a Lambda function.

37
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.850
The Lambda function can do an API call

38
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.840
using the AWS SDK to start a Batch job.

39
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.750
Option number two, we can push an event directly

40
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.090
from Amazon S3 into Amazon EventBridge

41
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.460
because EventBridge can directly also read

42
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.560
these Amazon S3 event notifications.

43
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.310
And EventBridge has a direct integration with Amazon ECS.

44
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.500
So the benefit of the step

45
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.020
is that you have more filtering capability

46
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.640
as part of Amazon EventBridge,

47
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.560
and you have a serverless offering

48
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.540
because you don't need to manage any kind of code.

49
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.040
EventBridge knows how to invoke AWS Batch.

50
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.720
Okay, so once the Batch job is invoked,

51
00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.860
what's going to happen?

52
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.870
Well, a Docker image is going to be pulled

53
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.930
by your Batch job from Amazon ECR, for example,

54
00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.700
and then your code will run.

55
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.340
So your code may need to retrieve the file

56
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.990
from Amazon S3 using an API call.

57
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.960
So you need to make sure that this job

58
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.880
has a correct IAM role, of course.

59
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.290
And then once the file is retrieved,

60
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.270
it's going to be processed and sent

61
00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.240
to the target Amazon S3 bucket destination.

62
00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.520
And then finally, maybe we can insert some metadata

63
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.020
into Amazon DynamoDB.

64
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.490
So for all these things, you need to make sure

65
00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.800
that the IAM role is correct.

66
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.780
So what is the difference between Lambda and Batch?

67
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.180
Well, Lambda gives you a time limit.

68
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.350
You have limited runtime,

69
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.288
so you get access to the built-in runtimes,

70
00:02:42.288 --> 00:02:45.330
or you can get special container Docker images

71
00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.700
built specifically for AWS Lambda,

72
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.950
but not all kind of Docker images.

73
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.990
Lambda gives you temporary disk space,

74
00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.200
but in a limited fashion, and Lambda is serverless.

75
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.060
Whereas Batch, well, you have no time limits.

76
00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.190
So you can have a Batch job as long as you want.

77
00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.140
You can have any runtime as long as this package

78
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.760
has any kind of Docker image.

79
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.490
So you have more flexibility here as well.

80
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.320
It's going to rely on EBS

81
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.510
or the instance store for disk space,

82
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.280
or it relies on EC2 instances

83
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.990
that can be directly managed by AWS.

84
00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.320
Or if you wanna go serverless, it relies on Fargate.

85
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.153
So now let's talk

86
00:03:17.153 --> 00:03:19.020
about these compute environments in details.

87
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.750
So the first one is a managed compute environment.

88
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.870
And in this case, AWS Batch will manage the capacity

89
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.360
and the instance types within your environment.

90
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.600
And you can choose between On-Demand or Spot Instances,

91
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.690
and you can set a maximum price for Spot Instances.

92
00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.130
So that means that with this,

93
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.080
we don't need to worry too much about the capacity.

94
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.860
It is Amazon's job to scale that capacity for us,

95
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.260
and we hopefully get good pricing as well.

96
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.600
So this is of a more managed alternative,

97
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.270
and therefore, it's called a managed compute environment.

98
00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.040
All the instances are going to be launched

99
00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.600
within your own VPC.

100
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.610
So you need to make sure that if you launch your instances

101
00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.230
in your own private subnets,

102
00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.690
it needs to have access to the ECS service.

103
00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.730
And so that means either you have a NAT gateway

104
00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.490
or a NAT instance to get access to the ECS service

105
00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.820
for the public route, or you need to create a VPC endpoint

106
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.270
for your ECS service to access it privately.

107
00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.550
Next, we have the unmanaged compute environment

108
00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.210
for EC2, ECS, and EKS in which you're going to control

109
00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.950
and manage your own compute resources,

110
00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.633
provisioning, and scaling.

111
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:20.970
Okay, so let's have a look

112
00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.680
into the managed compute environment in details

113
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.630
because that is the important one.

114
00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.100
So what do we have?

115
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.040
Well, we have Batch and we set how many min

116
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.000
or max vCPU we want to have in our compute environment

117
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.607
and if you want Spot Instances.

118
00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:38.610
And for example, the spot instances will launch m5.large,

119
00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.460
c5.xlarge, r5.2xlarge.

120
00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.960
So this doesn't matter really the type

121
00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.370
of instance that gets launched.

122
00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.300
What really matters is how many min and max vCPU we've set.

123
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.749
And then we'll have all our instances

124
00:04:49.749 --> 00:04:52.153
summed up together for the vCPU,

125
00:04:52.153 --> 00:04:54.420
and we'll see if that fits

126
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.490
our Batch managed compute environments.

127
00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.380
So then we have a Batch Job Queue,

128
00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.780
and this is how the jobs are being distributed

129
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.520
to our EC2 instances

130
00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.320
that will be running the Docker containers.

131
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.115
And so to send a job to AWS Batch,

132
00:05:07.115 --> 00:05:09.900
the one thing you have to do is to use the SDK

133
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.910
to add a job to the job queue.

134
00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.160
But this is something you can do with Lambda, obviously,

135
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.370
or CloudWatch events can do this for loop

136
00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.160
or step function can do this for you as well.

137
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.590
And then finally, in case you have many, many jobs

138
00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:25.380
in the Batch Job Queue, you can set up autoscaling,

139
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.270
so through the managed compute environment,

140
00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.820
to automatically create new Spot Instances

141
00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.800
or new On-Demand instances to respond

142
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.660
to the increase or decrease in jobs.

143
00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.870
So you have a really good, well-fitted environment

144
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.660
based on the size of your job queue.

145
00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.900
Okay, finally, I said that one Batch job

146
00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.360
usually equates to one EC2 instance.

147
00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.700
That's not the case when you have a multi-node mode.

148
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.980
So this is a newer mode called multi-node,

149
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:50.813
and this is good ones.

150
00:05:50.813 --> 00:05:55.140
You want to do large scale high performance computing, HPC.

151
00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.977
And so in this case, you're going to leverage multiple EC2

152
00:05:57.977 --> 00:06:01.440
or ECS instances at the same time to run your job.

153
00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.870
So it's really good when you have tightly coupled workloads

154
00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.240
and you need to have multiple instances at the same time

155
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.270
to share that workload to process it faster.

156
00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.220
So this represents a single job

157
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.160
and you specify how many nodes to create for that one job.

158
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.930
And so you're going to have one main node

159
00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.060
that will manage all the other nodes,

160
00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.030
and the other nodes are going to be called child nodes.

161
00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.400
So it does not work with Spot Instances for now,

162
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.477
and it works better if your EC2 launch mode

163
00:06:25.477 --> 00:06:27.450
is in the placement group cluster

164
00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.060
because you're going to get enhanced networking out of it.

165
00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.010
So very important, multi-node does not work

166
00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.200
with Spot Instances, and it's better to use

167
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.360
a cluster mode for placement group.

168
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.220
So we have a placement group of cluster mode.

169
00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.140
That means that everything is going to be on the same rack

170
00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.180
within the same availability zone,

171
00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.196
and Batch is going to submit a multi-node job.

172
00:06:45.196 --> 00:06:48.210
And so there's going to be one EC2 main instance

173
00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.640
that will be launched, and that EC2 instance

174
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.340
will launch and manage other EC2 instances

175
00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.800
within your multi-node job to perform the job,

176
00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.660
and then everything will be shut down.

177
00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.670
So that's all you need to know for Batch.

178
00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.670
Hopefully, that was very helpful and new knowledge for you,

179
00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.620
and I will see you in the next lecture.

